          "id": "c1",
          "value": {
            "language": "en",
            "text": "Montesquieu University – Bordeaux IV, or Université Montesquieu, was a public French university based in Pessac, a suburb of Bordeaux. It was also known as Bordeaux IV and was named after the French lawyer and philosopher Montesquieu. Its academic specialities included law, political science, economics and management.\n\nThe university succeeded the former Faculty of Law and Economics, whose origins are described as reaching back to the fifteenth century. That earlier history is distinct from the university’s own 1995 inception. In 2014, Bordeaux IV merged with Bordeaux 1 and Bordeaux 2 to form the University of Bordeaux. Wikidata records its dissolution on 1 January 2014 and identifies the University of Bordeaux as its replacement. It also records the PRES University of Bordeaux as its parent from 2007 to 2014.\n\nThe university was organised into six departments covering economics and management, law, and economic and social administration. It also included an Institute of Business Administration and two University Institutes of Technology; the Bordeaux Institute of Political Studies was annexed to it. Its teaching sites included Bordeaux and satellite sites in Agen and Périgueux. Wikidata records 17,635 students in 2013; the source does not specify a headcount or full-time-equivalent basis."
          },
